In article <110293@hydra.gatech.EDU>, mb20@prism.gatech.EDU (BRANCH,MICHAEL B) writes:
|>  Basically, what are some good general "philosophies" taken in approach
|> to moving through the first 8 - 10 levels? Should one try to cover every     
|> inch, or is it better to move ahead a bit quicker? 


Well, I'm not neccesarily the most qualified person to answer this, but I can offer 
the following basic strategies, and then our other Gentle Netters can agree or
disagree...

-----------Possible Spoilers--------












1) stay on levels awhile, and wait for more monsters. 
    An individual level will have more monsters appear, and you can get more 
    equipment from them as well as experience, etc.

2) Throw things
    You can hit a monster several times with rocks, knives, etc. you throw
    at them to "soften them up" a bit; sometimes you can even kill them before
    they get to you.  You can throw things down the hallway ahead of you,
    and if you get a "You miss it" or "You hit it" you know something is coming
    down the hall.  Use auto-pickup while you're doing this, 'cause then you get
    your items back as you go.

3) Be religious
    Sacrifice early and often if you find an altar

4) Run away
    If you are below maximum hit points and see a monster, run away.  Only
   attack if you have to or at maximum hit points.  Running in circles around a
   boulder, etc works really well if you keep the monster on the other side

5) Make  and use traps
    If you have a pick-axe you can make pits in strategic locations. If you
   zig-zag around traps while running away monsters will often fall in.  If a monster
   stumbles into a sleeping gas trap while following you they are easy pickin's.
   Some monsters will even fall for the same trap twice :-).
